RAID Library
============

This directory contains the Galois Field GF(2^8) erasure coding engine used by
SnapRAID. It provides multi-parity generation, missing data and parity block
recovery, error candidate validation, and brute-force block failure scanning.

The library supports up to 251 data blocks and up to 6 parity blocks using an
Extended Cauchy Matrix construction that guarantees MDS (Maximum Distance
Separable) properties for all supported combinations of disk failures.


Public API
----------

Header: #include "raid.h"

Before invoking any generation or recovery functions, call raid_init() once to
detect CPU capabilities and initialize mode dispatch. Then select the RAID mode
to use, if different from the default, and call raid_selftest(). raid_selftest()
verifies only the currently selected mode and is intended to be run at every
library startup before normal RAID operations.

  void raid_init(void)
    Initializes algorithm tables and detects hardware SIMD instruction sets
    (SSE2, SSSE3, AVX2, AVX-512, GFNI, NEON). Sets default operating mode to
    RAID_MODE_CAUCHY_RAID. Must be called before any other API function.

  int raid_mode(int mode)
    Sets or queries the active operating mode.
    - Pass RAID_MODE_GET (-1) to query current active mode.
    - Pass RAID_MODE_CAUCHY_RAID, RAID_MODE_VANDERMONDE_RAID, or
      RAID_MODE_CAUCHY_AES to set mode.
    Returns the mode active prior to this call.

  void raid_zero(void *zero)
    Registers a read-only buffer filled with zeros of block size 'size'.
    The buffer must be aligned to a 64-byte boundary. Required prior to
    calling raid_rec() or raid_data() for delta parity computation.

  void raid_gen(int nd, int np, size_t size, void **v, int streaming)
    Computes 'np' parity blocks from 'nd' data blocks. Vector 'v' contains
    'nd + np' block pointers (data blocks v[0..nd-1], parity blocks
    v[nd..nd+np-1]). Each block must be aligned to a 64-byte boundary.
    Data blocks are read-only; parity blocks are written. If 'streaming' is
    non-zero, non-temporal streaming stores are used where supported to avoid
    polluting the CPU cache when parity is written directly to disk. If
    'streaming' is zero, normal cached stores are used.

  void raid_rec(int nr, int *ir, int nd, int np, size_t size, void **v)
    Recovers 'nr' failed blocks (data or parity) listed in sorted index array
    'ir'. Indexes start at 0 (data 0..nd-1, parity nd..nd+np-1). Each block
    pointed to by 'v' must be aligned to a 64-byte boundary. Pointers in 'v'
    for good blocks are read; pointers for bad blocks are target destinations.
    Requires nr <= np.

  void raid_data(int nr, int *id, int *ip, int nd, size_t size, void **v)
    Recovers 'nr' failed data blocks listed in sorted array 'id', using
    parities listed in sorted array 'ip'. Each block pointed to by 'v' must
    be aligned to a 64-byte boundary. Parity blocks in 'v' are unmodified.
    Requires nr <= nd and nr <= RAID_PARITY_MAX.

  int raid_check(int nr, int *ir, int nd, int np, size_t size, void **v)
    Validates whether candidate failed block set 'ir' matches available parity
    redundancy. Each block pointed to by 'v' must be aligned to a 64-byte
    boundary. Returns 0 if candidate set matches, or -1 if invalid. Requires
    nr < np. Does not modify data or parity blocks.

  int raid_scan(int *ir, int nd, int np, size_t size, void **v)
    Searches for up to 'np - 1' failed blocks using available redundancy.
    Candidates are tested with an increasing number of failures, and the
    first combination matching the redundancy is returned. If multiple
    combinations match, the first one encountered is returned without
    checking uniqueness. Each block pointed to by 'v' must be aligned to a
    64-byte boundary. Fills 'ir' with the matching block indexes and returns
    their number (0 if no error), or -1 if no compatible combination is found.

  int raid_selftest(void)
    Runs startup verification tests for parity generation, recovery, and
    scanning across parity levels supported by the currently selected mode.
    It is intended to be called at every library startup after the RAID mode
    has been selected, to verify the selected implementation on the current
    CPU, compiler, and ABI. If the mode is changed later, the self test should
    be run again before using the new mode. Returns 0 on success, < 0 on
    failure.


Memory, Alignment and Aliasing Requirements
-------------------------------------------

  - Vector Layout: In vector 'v', data blocks MUST precede parity blocks:
      v[0 ... nd-1]       -> Data blocks
      v[nd ... nd+np-1]   -> Parity blocks (PAR1, PAR2, ..., PAR6)

  - Block Size: Parameter 'size' MUST be a multiple of 64 bytes
    (size % 64 == 0).

  - Memory Alignment: All data and parity blocks pointed to by 'v[]'
    (as well as the zero buffer passed to raid_zero()) MUST be aligned to
    a 64-byte boundary. This requirement applies to each individual block
    buffer, not to the pointer array 'v' itself.

  - Buffer Aliasing: Entries in 'v[]' MAY point to the same block buffer.
    Blocks are processed from lower to higher offsets. For each processed
    byte or vector chunk, all required inputs are read before outputs are
    written. Parity outputs are written in increasing parity order.
    Consequently, when multiple parity outputs point to the same block,
    later parity outputs overwrite earlier ones.

    This guarantee applies to block aliasing (identical block pointers);
    arbitrary partial overlap between distinct block ranges is not supported.

  - Sorted Index Arrays: Index arrays ('ir', 'id', 'ip') MUST be strictly
    sorted in ascending order (e.g. ir[0] < ir[1] < ... < ir[nr-1]).


Operating Modes and Polynomials
-------------------------------

  RAID_MODE_CAUCHY_RAID (Default mode = 0)
    Uses Cauchy matrix generator with standard RAID polynomial 0x1d
    (x^8 + x^4 + x^3 + x^2 + 1, decimal 285, 0x11d) and primitive generator
    g = 2. Supports parities 1 to 6. Matches standard Linux RAID-6 (Anvin
    method) for 2-parity arrays and ensures on-disk compatibility. On CPUs
    supporting Intel GFNI, generation and recovery are accelerated using
    VGF2P8AFFINEQB with precomputed affine matrices for the RAID field.

  RAID_MODE_VANDERMONDE_RAID (Mode = 1)
    Legacy mode supporting up to 3 parities using standard polynomial 0x1d.
    Uses Cauchy for PAR1/PAR2 and Vandermonde matrix coefficients for PAR3
    (Z parity). Optimized for low-end CPUs without vector instructions.

  RAID_MODE_CAUCHY_AES (Mode = 2)
    Uses Cauchy matrix with AES polynomial 0x1b (x^8 + x^4 + x^3 + x + 1,
    0x11b) and primitive generator g = 3. On CPUs supporting Intel GFNI,
    generation and recovery can use VGF2P8MULB directly for multiplication
    in the AES field. This mode also provides a useful benchmark and
    reference implementation for comparison with RAID_MODE_CAUCHY_RAID,
    where fixed-coefficient multiplication in the 0x11d field is implemented
    with VGF2P8AFFINEQB and precomputed affine matrices. Relative performance
    depends on the CPU, backend, parity count, and workload. Parity data is
    incompatible with standard 0x11d polynomial arrays.

  Both Cauchy modes use the same Extended Cauchy construction with
  coefficients based on powers of their respective primitive generators.
  GFNI acceleration is available for both fields: the RAID field uses
  VGF2P8AFFINEQB for fixed-coefficient multiplication, while the AES field
  can use native VGF2P8MULB. Neither instruction should be assumed to be
  faster in general; relative performance is hardware and workload
  dependent.


Implementation Structure
------------------------

  raid.h        Public API declarations, macros, and mode constants.
  raid.c        Cauchy matrix solver, Gauss-Jordan inversion, delta generation.
  module.c      Algorithm registration, CPU detection, and dynamic dispatch.
  int.c         Portable C fallback implementation (8/32/64-bit word loops).
  tables.c      Precomputed GF(2^8) log/exp, multiplication, Cauchy tables.
  mktables.c    Generator for GF(2^8), Cauchy, and GFNI affine tables.
  internal.h    Private header, assertion macros (BUG_ON), alignment helpers.

  Platform-Specific Acceleration:
    sse2.c      x86 SSE2 (128-bit SIMD vectorization)
    ssse3.c     x86 SSSE3 (128-bit pshufb nibble-split multiplication)
    avx2.c      x86 AVX2 (256-bit vectorization)
    avx512.c    x86 AVX-512BW (512-bit vectorization)
    gfni.c      x86 GFNI generation/recovery (RAID affine and AES multiply)
    neon.c      AArch64 ARM NEON (128-bit SIMD vectorization)
    neon32.c    AArch32 ARM NEON (128-bit SIMD vectorization)


Testing and Benchmarks
----------------------

Standalone test applications located in raid/test/ provide verification and
performance evaluation:

  raid/test/selftest   Quick startup self-test exercising raid_selftest().
  raid/test/fulltest   Comprehensive test suite verifying parity generation and
                       tail recovery for 1..251 data disks, and all multi-disk
                       failure recovery combinations for up to TEST_COUNT disks
                       (default 24/32).
  raid/test/invtest    Matrix inversion test verifying non-singularity for all
                       square submatrices of the Cauchy matrix.
  raid/test/speedtest  Benchmark measuring single-thread throughput (MiB/s)
                       for parity generation and recovery across all SIMD
                       implementations.
